From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Moeini <a.moeny@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 15:20:00 +0330
Subject: [PATCH] fuse: use fuse_reply_data() in Read() for macFUSE FSKit compatibility

When mounting via macFUSE 5.x with the FSKit backend (-o backend=fskit),
libfuse3 negotiates FUSE_DARWIN_CAP_REPLY_BUF. Replying with
fuse_reply_buf() sends the read payload inline over
MFChannelSendMessage, which fails with EIO for read requests of 128 KiB
or larger (e.g., Finder copies, cp/fcopyfile, and cat).

Using fuse_reply_data() for non-empty replies routes the payload through
fuse_reply_data_mfch() / MFMessageGetReplyBuffer on macOS while keeping
standard libfuse3 behavior on Linux.

Signed-off-by: Moeini <a.moeny@gmail.com>
---
 fuse/fuse.cpp | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/fuse/fuse.cpp b/fuse/fuse.cpp
index 08dbcf8..7b1c2d9 100644
--- a/fuse/fuse.cpp
+++ b/fuse/fuse.cpp
@@ -603,7 +603,17 @@ namespace
 					data = _session->GetPartialObject(objectId, begin, rsize);
 			}
 			mtp::debug("read ", data.size(), " bytes of data");
-			FUSE_CALL(fuse_reply_buf(req, static_cast<char *>(static_cast<void *>(data.data())), data.size()));
+			if (data.empty())
+			{
+				FUSE_CALL(fuse_reply_buf(req, nullptr, 0));
+				return;
+			}
+			struct fuse_bufvec bufv = {};
+			bufv.count = 1;
+			bufv.buf[0].size = data.size();
+			bufv.buf[0].mem = data.data();
+			bufv.buf[0].fd = -1;
+			FUSE_CALL(fuse_reply_data(req, &bufv, FUSE_BUF_NO_SPLICE));
 		}
 
 		void Write(fuse_req_t req, FuseId inode, const char *buf, size_t size, off_t off, struct fuse_file_info *fi)
-- 
